  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nGradual ridging continues over the Northern Rockies and into\nNortheast Montana, keeping conditions warm and dry. Winds remain \nlight and variable through today but are expected to increase \nduring the afternoon hours on Thursday. A lake wind advisory has \nbeen issued for Fort Peck Lake with east winds 15 to 25 mph and \ngusts up to 30 mph expected. \n\nA low pressure system continues to move south into the northern\nCalifornian region and is being closed off by a gradual northern \nstream trough ejecting eastward. The disturbance will lift into\nMontana over the weekend, bringing chances of showers and \nthunderstorms through Sunday. Scattered showers will be more \nconcentrated along the MT/ND border on Friday morning, then become\nmore widespread across the region by Friday evening through the \nweekend. \n\nCurrent model guidance indicate 0.50\" to 1.00\" of\ntotal rainfall, but the latest NBM run highlights eastern zones of\nthe CWA exceeding an inch of rain. The 90th percentile of the NBM\nshows much of Northeastern Montana seeing over two inches of\nrainfall. Localized heavy rain will be possible in places where \nembedded thunderstorms develop. The Weather Prediction Center has \nhighlighted a marginal risk of excessive rainfall for Friday\nthrough Saturday.\n\nWeak ridging will build on the backside of the departing low on\nMonday, allowing for drying conditions and an increase in\ntemperatures going into next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Confidence in rain chances this weekend are moderate and\n  increasing across the CWA, although there is less confidence in\n  northwestern zones.  \n\n- Confidence in amounts is increasing, with embedded thunderstorms\n  creating localized heavier rain in some areas. However, there\n  are still some uncertainties on where thunderstorms will\n  develop. \n\nKENZ",
